          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My HbA1c came back at 7.2%. What does that mean, what target does guidance describe, and which code would type 2 diabetes be recorded under?",
          "answer": "A 7.2% HbA1c is a blood test that reflects your average blood sugar over roughly the past two to three months, not a single moment. The screening guidance (*Type 2 diabetes: who is screened, and the diagnostic thresholds*) places 5.7% to 6.4% in the prediabetes range, so 7.2% is above that range. The sources I have did not state the exact diabetes cut-off, so I can't tell you from them whether your result meets it. Your clinician interprets this value, usually alongside other tests or a repeat test. The screening guidance also notes that HbA1c can be less reliable with certain hemoglobin variants, recent blood transfusion, iron-deficiency anemia, pregnancy, and advanced kidney disease. If any of these apply to you, tell your clinician.\n\nDiabetes association guidance (*Type 2 diabetes: glycemic targets and monitoring intervals*) describes an HbA1c below 7% as a reasonable goal for many adults who are not pregnant. The goal is meant to reduce complications affecting the eyes, kidneys and nerves. A stricter goal, such as below 6.5%, may suit some people if it can be reached without significant low blood sugar, and a less strict goal may suit others. Your result is slightly above the general 7% figure, but whether that matters for you is a question for your clinician. The targets document notes that authorities disagree on this target, but the passage I retrieved did not say which authorities differ or how, so I can't report that difference. The evidence that tight blood sugar control alone protects large blood vessels is weaker, which is why blood pressure and cholesterol are managed alongside it.\n\nIn the US version of ICD-10 (ICD-10-CM), type 2 diabetes is recorded under code E11, in the endocrine, nutritional and metabolic chapter (*Coding type 2 diabetes: E11*). If a record says only \"diabetes mellitus\" without a type, the system defaults to type 2. Taking insulin does not change the code. Clinicians and billing staff assign codes, so you don't need to act on this. Codes can differ between versions of the system.\n\nPlease discuss the result with your clinician. Ask what it means for you, whether a repeat test is needed, and what target is appropriate for your situation. If you have symptoms such as extreme thirst, frequent urination, or confusion or vomiting, contact a clinician promptly.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
